The array stores the balance after each character. An opening parenthesis adds one and a closing parenthesis subtracts one. No stored balance may be negative, and the last balance must be zero.

Dry run for `())(` gives balances `1, 0, -1, 0`. The negative value at position three is enough to reject the string, even though the final count returns to zero.

```javascript
class Solution {
    isValid(str) {
        const balances = [];
        let balance = 0;

        for (const ch of str) {
            balance += ch === '(' ? 1 : -1;
            balances.push(balance);
        }

        for (const value of balances) {
            if (value < 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return balance === 0;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because all prefix balances are stored.

The recursive parser reads the string from left to right. When it sees `(`, it starts parsing a nested group. When it sees `)`, it closes the current group. A closing parenthesis at the top level, or an unfinished group at the end, makes the input invalid.

This uses the call stack instead of an explicit stack. Very deeply nested input can therefore use `O(n)` stack space.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public bool IsValid(string str) {
        int index = 0;
        return Parse(str, ref index, false) && index == str.Length;
    }

    private bool Parse(string str, ref int index, bool insideGroup) {
        while (index < str.Length) {
            if (str[index] == '(') {
                index++;
                if (!Parse(str, ref index, true)) {
                    return false;
                }
            } else {
                if (!insideGroup) {
                    return false;
                }

                index++;
                return true;
            }
        }

        return !insideGroup;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since each character is consumed once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case from recursive calls.

A stack keeps the opening brackets that still need a match. For every `(`, push it. For every `)`, there must be an opening bracket available, so pop one. The string is valid only when the stack is empty at the end.

Dry run for `(()())`:
- `(`, stack size becomes 1
- `(`, stack size becomes 2
- `)`, stack size becomes 1
- `(`, stack size becomes 2
- `)`, stack size becomes 1
- `)`, stack size becomes 0

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isValid(string str) {
        stack<char> openings;

        for (char ch : str) {
            if (ch == '(') {
                openings.push(ch);
            } else {
                if (openings.empty()) {
                    return false;
                }
                openings.pop();
            }
        }

        return openings.empty();
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case when all characters are opening parentheses.

Since the input contains only parentheses, a full stack is not needed. The counter stores how many unmatched opening parentheses are currently active. A closing parenthesis lowers it. If the value ever becomes negative, a closing parenthesis appeared too early.

An empty string is valid because it has no unmatched parentheses. A string with leftover openings is rejected by the final check.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isValid(String str) {
        int openCount = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
            if (str.charAt(i) == '(') {
                openCount++;
            } else {
                openCount--;
                if (openCount < 0) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return openCount == 0;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

