## Intuition
When checking whether brackets are valid, every closing bracket must match the most recently opened bracket.

For example, in {[()]}, the ) must match ( before we can match [ with ]. This last opened, first closed behavior is exactly what a stack provides.

So, we push every opening bracket onto the stack. Whenever we encounter a closing bracket, we check whether it matches the bracket at the top of the stack.
## Approach
1. Store all opening brackets in an unordered_set for quick identification.
2. Store each closing bracket and its corresponding opening bracket in an unordered_map.
3. Traverse the string:
    ->If the current character is an opening bracket, push it onto the stack.
    ->Otherwise, check:
        -> The stack is not empty.
        ->The top of the stack matches the required opening bracket.
    ->If both conditions hold, pop the stack.
    ->Otherwise, the string is invalid.
4. After processing the entire string, return true only if the stack is empty.

This handles both incorrect bracket order and unmatched opening brackets.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
   public:
    bool isValid(const string& str) {
        const unordered_set<char> opens = {'[', '{', '('};
        const unordered_map<char, char> opposite = {
            {']', '['} , 
            {')', '('} ,
            {'}', '{'}
        };

        stack<char> st;
        for (char bracket : str) {
            if (opens.find(bracket) != opens.end())
                st.push(bracket);
            else {
                if (!st.empty() && opposite.find(bracket)->second == st.top())
                    st.pop();
                else
                    return false;
            }
        }

        return st.empty();
    }
};
```

